{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534073-why-does-assamite-skin-turn-darker-with-age","id":1534073,"title":"Why does Assamite skin turn darker with age?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-04-11T19:18:49-07:00","id":1534073,"title":"Why does Assamite skin turn darker with age?","content":"Hello here!<br /> <br /> It's well known that all of Haqim's children's skin turns ebony over the centuries, except for al-Ashrad's. The same happens to Abyss Mystics who use the <i>Drinking the Blood of Ahriman (</i>DA Guide to the High Clans) / <i>Claiming the Dark </i>(V20 Lore of the Clans) ritual and stay in it for too long. Has the reason for the darkening of the skin ever been explained?<br /> <br /> Thank you in advance for your answers!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-11T21:17:45-07:00","id":1534076,"title":"","content":"Out of Universe, Because they wanted to make them cool and orientalist.<br /> <br /> According to the developers of 5th Edition, that's no longer the case for obvious reasons.<br /> <br /> In-Universe, you might assume it was just a by-product of their Blood Magic. I doubt Caine would curse them like 2nd Edition Drow.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-11T23:46:22-07:00","id":1534084,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534073-why-does-assamite-skin-turn-darker-with-age?p=1534076#post1534076\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Out of Universe, Because they wanted to make them cool and orientalist.<br /> <br /> According to the developers of 5th Edition, that's no longer the case for obvious reasons.<br /> <br /> In-Universe, you might assume it was just a by-product of their Blood Magic. I doubt Caine would curse them like 2nd Edition Drow.</div> </div> </div> </div>Why their Blood Magic? What's it got to do with the ebony skin?<br /> As an aside... When I read <i>Revelations of the Dark Mother</i> and Lilith cursing Assamites for stealing the seeds of her Garden (or stealing something, can't remember what), it made me think that Dur-An-Ki was a byproduct of that theft - which is interesting since it's supposed to be THE oldest Blood Magic. I need to read the Bahari chapter in the TalMaheRa book, but I notice it mentions ashipus - like what Dur-An-Ki practitioners are referred to as. Do they make the connection Dur-An-Ki-Lilith solid there?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-12T01:50:23-07:00","id":1534089,"title":"","content":"There isn't an official reason.<br /> <br /> As noted, like the rest of the Independent Clans, they started with a lot of problematic baggage based in unintentional offensive ethnic/racial/religious stereotyping. WW learned, after a few rounds of it, that trying to &quot;fix&quot; this with in-universe explanations tends not to actually fix the problem. So depending on the design intent of a given book, later books either do their best to ignore such things, or fix them by doing the only thing that can: get rid of them (though that can still be done poorly).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-12T02:24:16-07:00","id":1534090,"title":"","content":"Not even the Banu Haqim know the reason - tho I have VERY faint memories the 1st edition Claanbook mentioned soemthing regarding this, but honestly not too sure about it nor have it with me at the moment<br /> Al-Ashrad was the only &quot;pale&quot; Assamite, direct child of Ur-Shulgi (later a possible reference or hint, the Sheperd was actually a re-embraced Baali )<br /> <br /> As for the why... <br /> I think - puting aside the fact it looks cool - it potentially could be explained with two things:<br /> With a darker and darker skin it is progressively easier to hide in the shadows and the Children of Haqim was the Clan of the Hunt.<br /> So simply a natural trait passed down by Haqim via the Blood<br /> <br /> The second is a bit more... esoteric and complicated. And of course it is just my headcanon<br /> The Children of Haqim darkening skin color is personification of the darkening of their souls.<br /> Their skin grown darker with age and power, and over time it slowly changes to obsidian black. <br /> Why? This is where some mythology comes in.<br /> Jinn and Angels are the supernatural creatures of Almighty Allah. Unlike humans, jinn are the invisible beings, and humans have no such powers to see them, they are invisible to naked human eye. (like the members of a certain clan)<br /> The jinn are made of smokeless fire, while angels made of light,and they were created before jinn, but the Jinn inherited Earth first before humans who were created after them<br /> Snakes are the animals most frequently associated with jinn. Black snakes are commonly believed to be evil jinn<br /> So with a little paralel, the Hunters who are &quot;above Humans&quot; and unclean because of the blood consumption and murders are could be linked to the Jinn - maybe one is repsonsible for the Darkening","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-12T02:34:02-07:00","id":1534091,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534073-why-does-assamite-skin-turn-darker-with-age?p=1534090#post1534090\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not even the Banu Haqim know the reason - tho I have VERY faint memories the 1st edition Claanbook mentioned soemthing regarding this, but honestly not too sure about it nor have it with me at the moment<br /> Al-Ashrad was the only &quot;pale&quot; Assamite, direct child of Ur-Shulgi (later a possible reference or hint, the Sheperd was actually a re-embraced Baali )<br /> <br /> As for the why...<br /> I think - puting aside the fact it looks cool - it potentially could be explained with two things:<br /> With a darker and darker skin it is progressively easier to hide in the shadows and the Children of Haqim was the Clan of the Hunt.<br /> So simply a natural trait passed down by Haqim via the Blood<br /> <br /> The second is a bit more... esoteric and complicated. And of course it is just my headcanon<br /> The Children of Haqim darkening skin color is personification of the darkening of their souls.<br /> Their skin grown darker with age and power, and over time it slowly changes to obsidian black.<br /> Why? This is where some mythology comes in.<br /> Jinn and Angels are the supernatural creatures of Almighty Allah. Unlike humans, jinn are the invisible beings, and humans have no such powers to see them, they are invisible to naked human eye. (like the members of a certain clan)<br /> The jinn are made of smokeless fire, while angels made of light,and they were created before jinn, but the Jinn inherited Earth first before humans who were created after them<br /> Snakes are the animals most frequently associated with jinn. Black snakes are commonly believed to be evil jinn<br /> So with a little paralel, the Hunters who are &quot;above Humans&quot; and unclean because of the blood consumption and murders are could be linked to the Jinn - maybe one is repsonsible for the Darkening</div> </div> </div> </div>Cool explanation. And for the record, in the real world, Middle Eastern and African vampire myths fall under the jinn rubric. They're even occasionally discussed in some Quranic commentaries. That's why it's my default take in VtM and Chronicles of Darkness alike that \u0130slamicate vampires regard themselves as jinn.<br /> The only discrepancy is that from the point of view of said Quranic commentaries, nobody dies and then resurrects as a vampire: it's just a jinn who takes on the visage of a deceased person to fool and deceive people and get easier access to blood. So that messes up with the Embrace idea, and requires some change in game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-12T10:23:41-07:00","id":1534098,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534073-why-does-assamite-skin-turn-darker-with-age?p=1534084#post1534084\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Why their Blood Magic? What's it got to do with the ebony skin?</div> </div> </div> </div>Their magic being based on concentrating and accumulating vitae while the Tremere curse was in effect.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-12T14:14:19-07:00","id":1534106,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534073-why-does-assamite-skin-turn-darker-with-age?p=1534098#post1534098\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Their magic being based on concentrating and accumulating vitae while the Tremere curse was in effect.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sorry, \u0130'm still not following.<br /> 1) the skin condition exists from long before the Tremere curse;<br /> 2) Dur-An-Ki is not based on concentrating and accumulating vitae, but on spirit contracts. They do have a well in Alamut with the blood of the whole clan, which is used sympathetically for some of the sorcerers' practices to keep the clan connected accross long distances, but \u0130 wouldn't say that the well is the basis of their magic at all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-12T15:11:31-07:00","id":1534110,"title":"","content":"I recently picked up the early version of the Followers of Set and Assamites and they are definitely a product of the time and having stereotypes that would raise eyebrows. I wonder what the Malkavian book of the time was like when it comes to depictions of mental illness.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-12T16:33:35-07:00","id":1534113,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534073-why-does-assamite-skin-turn-darker-with-age?p=1534106#post1534106\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Sorry, \u0130'm still not following.<br /> 1) the skin condition exists from long before the Tremere curse;<br /> 2) Dur-An-Ki is not based on concentrating and accumulating vitae, but on spirit contracts. They do have a well in Alamut with the blood of the whole clan, which is used sympathetically for some of the sorcerers' practices to keep the clan connected accross long distances, but \u0130 wouldn't say that the well is the basis of their magic at all.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm sorry, I thought you wanted an explanation in-universe for something that should never have been true.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-12T22:46:23-07:00","id":1534120,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534073-why-does-assamite-skin-turn-darker-with-age?p=1534113#post1534113\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm sorry, I thought you wanted an explanation in-universe for something that should never have been true.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u0130 thought it had already been established through the various replies that there was no such an explanation in the manuals. I am very happy to hear people's headcanons and some idea might be really cool. You presented an idea, though, that rested on two premises which were both inaccurate lore wise. I questioned that politely. I don't see why the sarcasm would be justified.<br /> <br /> \u0130t's true that the independent clans are heavily stereotypised. But out of all issues, I don't really see the fact that one clan has supernaturally darkening skin (different from human darker skin tones) as being the problem. That can be turned into a cool feature with a cool reason. If we need to fix stereotypes, the fact that the Ashirra are led by the Assamites and Lasombra and the way both clans are characterised through all editions is way more of a problem than the Assamite's skin condition, if they're taken to represent a big part of Middle Eastern culture.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-13T03:44:08-07:00","id":1534123,"title":"","content":"I\u2019m glad they got rid of the supernaturally darkening skin for the Banu Haqim in V5. When I think about it, that does seem kind of Orientalist.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-13T05:15:28-07:00","id":1534126,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534073-why-does-assamite-skin-turn-darker-with-age?p=1534091#post1534091\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Cool explanation. And for the record, in the real world, Middle Eastern and African vampire myths fall under the jinn rubric. They're even occasionally discussed in some Quranic commentaries. That's why it's my default take in VtM and Chronicles of Darkness alike that \u0130slamicate vampires regard themselves as jinn.<br /> The only discrepancy is that from the point of view of said Quranic commentaries, nobody dies and then resurrects as a vampire: it's just a jinn who takes on the visage of a deceased person to fool and deceive people and get easier access to blood. So that messes up with the Embrace idea, and requires some change in game.</div> </div> </div> </div>It does warrant mention, jinn as a cultural phenomenon predates Islam, and its presence within Islam is probably down to assimilating Arabic polytheism. Kind of like all that Zoroastrianism, which Muslim scholars and clergy hilariously refuse to admit to this day. So there's that, to preemptively counter any &quot;yeah but Islam is a newer religion, which BH adopted while Haqim and all the methuselahs were asleep&quot; argument.<br /> <br /> With regards to the Embrace, there's no conclusive canonical explanation as to the metaphysics of it. The best one can say of it is &quot;vampires rationalize it based on the historio-cultural context in which they lived&quot;. Saying the mortal's soul departs and the body's possessed by a jinn is just as valid as any other explanation, and it still explains the Beast.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-13T06:12:27-07:00","id":1534127,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534073-why-does-assamite-skin-turn-darker-with-age?p=1534126#post1534126\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It does warrant mention, jinn as a cultural phenomenon predates Islam, and its presence within Islam is probably down to assimilating Arabic polytheism. Kind of like all that Zoroastrianism, which Muslim scholars and clergy hilariously refuse to admit to this day. So there's that, to preemptively counter any &quot;yeah but Islam is a newer religion, which BH adopted while Haqim and all the methuselahs were asleep&quot; argument.<br /> <br /> With regards to the Embrace, there's no conclusive canonical explanation as to the metaphysics of it. The best one can say of it is &quot;vampires rationalize it based on the historio-cultural context in which they lived&quot;. Saying the mortal's soul departs and the body's possessed by a jinn is just as valid as any other explanation, and it still explains the Beast.</div> </div> </div> </div>Jinn do predate \u0130slam (in the sense of the specific religious form brought by Prophet Muhammad - peace and blessings be upon him - in the 7th century). The Quran simply frames part of pre-\u0130slamic polytheim as jinn-worship, pointing out that humans through history have variously deviated from the prophets' calls to monotheism by worshipping angels, jinn, idols, celestial bodies, stone, etc.<br /> Jinn is also a catch all category for any monster or supernatural being other than angels, so stories of creatures we know from other cultures were at times also present among the Arabs as jinn-stories, like the stories of alluring women with part animal (be it hooves or snake features) traits killing men or kidnapping children. Jinn also have many tribes and forms. Many Muslims (like myself) have no issue hearing a story from another part of the world and thinking &quot;oh, these must be the local jinn&quot;.<br /> <br /> As for the Zoroastrian influence on \u0130slam, again, the Quranic narrative is that God sent prophets to all but only mentioned some by name in the Quran, and then people deviated in various ways from the laws assigned to them. So there is nothing surprising when a story or idea also found elsewhere is supported by the Quran or other \u0130slamic sources, since some notions are true and were already taught before. Many Muslims - religious scholars included - would concede that Zoroaster was likely a prophet, but since he was not mentioned by name in the Quran, it would be presumptuous and a lie against God to say that he definitely was a prophet. \u0130f \u0130 recall correctly, Harut and Marut (two figures in the Quran) are also part of Zoroastrian myth.<br /> Zoroastrian traditions also had a specific influence on \u0130slamicate spirituality and esotericism stemming from central Asia, particularly around the symbolism of light and darkness, but that's a bit more complex to trace historically, and, yes, it's possible that the average religious scholar lacks the type of academic background to be aware of these influences, though some are (\u0130 came across this myself in my research for my MA thesis). I don't think it's religiously significant though. \u0130slamic spirituality was expressed in ways that make sense to the local culture while still being rooted in the Quran. Muslims in Andalus occasionally said things using Christian symbolism, for example, and the same happened in areas that were previously prevalently Zoroastrian. Given the recognition of previous prophets and religious forms, it's not that big of a deal, except among ignorants of this dimension of the religion.<br /> That being said, \u0130ranian Muslim scholars in particular may be extra sensitive to comparison to Zoroastrianism, as Indian and Pakistani Muslim scholars may be to comparison to Hinduism, because it has a particular impact on religious discussions and movements there. I am not well informed of what happens in those areas.<br /> <br /> Going back to the Assamites, if \u0130 get your point, \u0130 agree: frankly, \u0130slam is a late phenomenon in the history of the clan. Any characteristic of the clan should be rooted in much older traditions, like Mesopotamian culture (like Dur-An-Ki) and Persian culture (like the VDA20 Companion saying that most elder Assamites in Asia are still Zoroastrian, which was a nice touch). So in this sense, \u0130 wouldn't link the black skin to \u0130slam at all. But since jinn predate \u0130slam, referring to them in whatever reason one gives for the skin darkening \u0130 think is ok. But then it makes the Assamites very tied to the Arabian peninsula unless one uses other words for jinn,from other cultures, but that has complications. So, for this, \u0130 might favour other ideas.<br /> <br /> As for the Embrace and the Beast, the issue is that it's quite important to the setting that new vampires all across the world believe to be their former selves brought back to life. That's a big part of the psychological elements of the game - though more so in Chronicles of Darkness Requiem.<br /> \u0130 think a game where vampires only impersonate deceased humans, taking over their contacts, allies, resources, etc. would be very cool. A psychological residue of the deceased human could also be a sort of internal antagonist for the main characters, a sort of reverse Beast. Characters might struggle psychologically between the Beast pulsions natural to them but which must be controlled to succeed in the human world, and the human &quot;ghost&quot; trying to make you fail to protect their dear ones and reveal your monstrous nature. \u0130 think it's easy to run a game like this using VtM, though \u0130 don't think it's the same game. The ajustments to be made would be small, though, because VtM vampires even with Humanity are easily focused on vampire, rather than mortal, society, and multiple groups and Roads emphasise the difference between vampires (and their interests) and humans.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":16965,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","user":"Hermeticus","date":".  completed the answer 04-13-2024, 10:07 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-13T12:25:20-07:00","id":1534142,"title":"","content":"I thought it was just a peculiarity of the clan in which blood pigments concentrate in the skin causing this darker coloration as the years go by, or if you prefer the V20, a result of prolonged use of the Quietus discipline as it alters the composition of the blood itself or something like this. It looks functional at least.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27832-psicose","name":"Psicose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27832_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-13T13:12:55-07:00","id":1534145,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Psicose</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534073-why-does-assamite-skin-turn-darker-with-age?p=1534142#post1534142\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I thought it was just a peculiarity of the clan in which blood pigments concentrate in the skin causing this darker coloration as the years go by, or if you prefer the V20, a result of prolonged use of the Quietus discipline as it alters the composition of the blood itself or something like this. It looks functional at least.</div> </div> </div> </div>I like the Quietus-based explanation. It could be tweaked by making it so that the higher the Quietus rating the vampire has, the darker the skin, as a side effect of the blood altering its composition. But the clan would be very interested in keeping the link between these two things secret to avoid letting opponents know how poisonous a given Assamite is.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-13T13:13:33-07:00","id":1534146,"title":"","content":"That's the problem with an ongoing game series as you will get writers who will come up with such ideas because it's &quot;cool&quot; but not consider how it will fit later down the road. But it's something just adjust or ignore depending on your game..","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-13T16:24:30-07:00","id":1534166,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534073-why-does-assamite-skin-turn-darker-with-age?p=1534145#post1534145\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I like the Quietus-based explanation. It could be tweaked by making it so that the higher the Quietus rating the vampire has, the darker the skin, as a side effect of the blood altering its composition. But the clan would be very interested in keeping the link between these two things secret to avoid letting opponents know how poisonous a given Assamite is.</div> </div> </div> </div>Like everything in VTM or WOD in general, even the dark creatures don't know everything about the dark creatures, so I would say very few outside of the clan would know this and even if they do know it can be used to their advantage.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27832-psicose","name":"Psicose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27832_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-13T17:47:26-07:00","id":1534173,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534073-why-does-assamite-skin-turn-darker-with-age?p=1534127#post1534127\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Going back to the Assamites, if \u0130 get your point, \u0130 agree: frankly, \u0130slam is a late phenomenon in the history of the clan. Any characteristic of the clan should be rooted in much older traditions, like Mesopotamian culture (like Dur-An-Ki) and Persian culture (like the VDA20 Companion saying that most elder Assamites in Asia are still Zoroastrian, which was a nice touch). So in this sense, \u0130 wouldn't link the black skin to \u0130slam at all. But since jinn predate \u0130slam, referring to them in whatever reason one gives for the skin darkening \u0130 think is ok. But then it makes the Assamites very tied to the Arabian peninsula unless one uses other words for jinn,from other cultures, but that has complications. So, for this, \u0130 might favour other ideas.</div> </div> </div> </div>Without getting into the &quot;religious discussion&quot; side of things for obvious reasons, that's kind of the neat part...even though it doesn't necessarily track by lore. The reason I brought up Zoroastrianism relevant to this is, if I remember my Islamic studies right, jinn under the Quranic narrative still exist according to Zoroastrian duality -- at least in terms of Muslim and kafir jinn. Which, if one squints hard enough, would suggest BH skin color as directly correlative to degeneration, and only indirectly correlative to age as older vampires tend towards lower road/path ratings.<br /> <br /> Even then, I'd argue despite what the text says, character illustrations and individual descriptions tell a different tale. Every elder and methuselah with higher road/path ratings has been depicted with lighter skin, up to and including Tegyrius and Fatima. But, that appears even worse on the &quot;is this racist/culturalist?&quot; spectrum at face value, even though those characters remain devout Muslims and would still therefore abide by the &quot;good or bad jinn&quot; duality, however it's contextualized. Tying skin color to morality is just bad optics before a Western audience, no matter how well-vindicated it might be considering non-Western belief systems in their own context.<br /> <br /> As far as tying vampires to specific geographic regions, and cultures, I personally see no problem with it so long as those regions and cultures are represented with due diligence and respect. Nobody's out here starting flame wars over Tzimisce being a caricature of Slavic culture -- but on the other hand, Tzimisce aren't exactly being depicted as hardcore alcoholics who listen to hardbass and wear Adidas track suits, are they. However objectively hilarious a Tzimisce gopnik might be, or how badly I want to play one now that I've thought of it.<br /> <br /> <i>With</i> getting into the &quot;religious discussion&quot; side of things...yeah, if I remember right, it's mostly Shia and Sufist Muslims who take issue with pointing to Zoroastrian influences on Islam. If I remember the narrative as I heard it right, aspects of Zoroastrianism are regarded as divine revelation, but Zoroaster/Zarathustra himself is considered a false prophet. Which conveniently gets around the entire question of whether Zoroastrianism itself had influence over early Islam as an organized religion. Which to me personally is a non-question -- religions syncretize and assimilate regional belief systems as they grow.<br /> <br /> Hell, contemporary Catholicism and Protestantism bear a lot more semblance to European paganism (and in the US, post-Renaissance mercantilism and capitalism) in form and practice than Judaism...and Christianity is a religion that started as a Hebrew eschatonic cult.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-15T14:26:22-07:00","id":1534267,"title":"","content":"Thank god they removed that stupid idea from the setting<br /> It was just like if there was a Clan of Vampires that was compelled to lie, cheat and steal, and be &quot;very coincidentally&quot; depicted as entirely Romani and nomadic. <br /> <br /> I say, for anyone that want to keep some of the darkening idea, I suggest that they make all Haqimites have their veins (all over the body, face included) get really saturated and dense, and so becoming dark with blood, after they diablerize someone. And that last like the Gangrel beastening curse, or take a decade to subside. That for me fits very well with the whole idea of the Clan of Judges, like as if Haqim made them as such because &quot;they judge and execute fairly, and they aren't scared to show their judgment on their skin&quot;. They only diablerize the unworthy and criminal, and so like a judges toga or hammer, they carry it as part of the attire. <br /> And so, older Haqimites, who have diablerized a lot of vamps, would have maybe darker veins, but not the skin itself. <br /> That for me just makes more sense than just getting dark like silver cuz of age.<br /> Which basically only exists as a clan feature to have Ur-Shulgi be &quot;Oooooooh so different, he doesn't have dark sin, what come!?!?&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Strange... When coincidence seems too convenient, I prefer to call it fate.</i><br /> <br /> <b><sup>-Blood Omen: Legacy of Kain</sup> </b>d=","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25534-banu_saulot","name":"Banu_Saulot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25534_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"335"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-15T16:27:18-07:00","id":1534273,"title":"","content":"I thought Ashrad was the only member of the clan who skin never got dark with age, I didn't know that his sire had the same deal with what little flesh he has left","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-15T20:40:49-07:00","id":1534282,"title":"","content":"I thought I read somewhere that it was a result of their constant diablerie? Old memory so I'm not swearing by it, but I seem to recall reading that somewhere.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","name":"CajunKhan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3598_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1700"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-15T22:34:34-07:00","id":1534288,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534073-why-does-assamite-skin-turn-darker-with-age?p=1534173#post1534173\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Without getting into the &quot;religious discussion&quot; side of things for obvious reasons, that's kind of the neat part...even though it doesn't necessarily track by lore. The reason I brought up Zoroastrianism relevant to this is, if I remember my Islamic studies right, jinn under the Quranic narrative still exist according to Zoroastrian duality -- at least in terms of Muslim and kafir jinn. Which, if one squints hard enough, would suggest BH skin color as directly correlative to degeneration, and only indirectly correlative to age as older vampires tend towards lower road/path ratings.</div> </div> </div> </div>Jinn, like humans, are said to choose whether to obey or disobey God (though, like humans, their choice is part of the Divine Decree, since nothing is created outside of it). The same goes, according to some of the ancient philosophers, for the Greek daemons, which in fact have been compared to the jinn (but so have the fairies and elves of European folklore). Ibl\u012bs (the &quot;Devil&quot;, i.e. the boss one) was a pious jinn who rebelled to God when Adam was given a position above him, and since then he's been calling other jinn and humans to turn away from God, out of spite for Adam's descendants. \u0130slamic angels, by contrast, do not choose between obedience and disobedience, but simply do everything God commands them to.<br /> \u0130 don't know how that tracks with Zoroastrian doctrines, but even if it agrees, it would be what the Quran itself says, that it's not bringing new doctrines, but it agrees with previous revelations, except where they've been corrupted or where new, more accurate info is being offered.<br /> \u0130f you are saying that Banu Haqim, because of the darkening skin, are singled out among the jinn-vampires for being extra-evil, \u0130 wouldn't see that as clear-cut. \u0130t's true that in many languages and cultures darkening or turning black are used as a negative image (even in several African languages from what \u0130 am told by friends), but that's not a univocal symbol: black is also linked to nobility, even in Arabic. \u0130 remember going years ago to an Egyptian dream interpreter (who, should some think it's relevant, was not black) and relaying some dreams involving black men and him saying they represented nobility because black is the colour of nobility. \u0130 could go on on the ambivalence of the colour black in various symbolic and cultural systems, but all \u0130'm trying to say is that, regardless of the developers' intentions off game, players and Storytellers have plenty of range to reframe the Assamite darkening. \u0130 like the clan and tend to see them positively (\u0130 appreciate the ethics of keeping other vampires in check to prevent abuse of the kine), so in my games \u0130 would probably have Assamites think of their darkening as a mark of nobility and authority over others, while non-Assamites may think what they like - and maybe link it to Cain's curse, perhaps claiming they had a prominent role in the diablerie of the 2nd generation. Or Caine might have wanted to separate Haqim from his grandchildren because he sort of embraced himself when he killed the vampire king and queen according to one Assamite myth, so he cursed Haqim and his childer with darkening skin and weird auras.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534073-why-does-assamite-skin-turn-darker-with-age?p=1534173#post1534173\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Even then, I'd argue despite what the text says, character illustrations and individual descriptions tell a different tale. Every elder and methuselah with higher road/path ratings has been depicted with lighter skin, up to and including Tegyrius and Fatima. But, that appears even worse on the &quot;is this racist/culturalist?&quot; spectrum at face value, even though those characters remain devout Muslims and would still therefore abide by the &quot;good or bad jinn&quot; duality, however it's contextualized. Tying skin color to morality is just bad optics before a Western audience, no matter how well-vindicated it might be considering non-Western belief systems in their own context.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u0130 had never noticed differences in skin tone based on Roads. That would seem like a very silly decision of illustrators and whoever instructed them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534073-why-does-assamite-skin-turn-darker-with-age?p=1534173#post1534173\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As far as tying vampires to specific geographic regions, and cultures, I personally see no problem with it so long as those regions and cultures are represented with due diligence and respect. Nobody's out here starting flame wars over Tzimisce being a caricature of Slavic culture -- but on the other hand, Tzimisce aren't exactly being depicted as hardcore alcoholics who listen to hardbass and wear Adidas track suits, are they. However objectively hilarious a Tzimisce gopnik might be, or how badly I want to play one now that I've thought of it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fair enough.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534073-why-does-assamite-skin-turn-darker-with-age?p=1534173#post1534173\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><i>With</i> getting into the &quot;religious discussion&quot; side of things...yeah, if I remember right, it's mostly Shia and Sufist Muslims who take issue with pointing to Zoroastrian influences on Islam. If I remember the narrative as I heard it right, aspects of Zoroastrianism are regarded as divine revelation, but Zoroaster/Zarathustra himself is considered a false prophet. Which conveniently gets around the entire question of whether Zoroastrianism itself had influence over early Islam as an organized religion. Which to me personally is a non-question -- religions syncretize and assimilate regional belief systems as they grow.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u0130 never heard of Zoroaster being conclusively called a false prophet myself, but \u0130 can imagine some people - especially ignorant - saying that. \u0130t's usually recommended not to make decisive statements on these things.<br /> As for influences, as \u0130 said, \u0130slam is a bit weird because it actually speaks of previous religions - zoroastrianism included - in its original, oldest texts and does not reject everything. There is leeway, in fact, for accepting things that don't contradict what the Quran is teaching, and this itself would be a teaching of the Quran. So \u0130 find it weird when one describes it as syncretism, because it's actually something already available in the original religious system offered by the Quran and Hadith corpus, whether in terms of ideas or in terms of social practices.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-15T22:52:37-07:00","id":1534290,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25534-banu_saulot\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar25534_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"25534\" data-vbnamecard=\"25534\">Banu_Saulot</a> and <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar27361_11.jpg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"27361\" data-vbnamecard=\"27361\">Lysander</a> : as far as I recall, Ur-Shulgi has super black skin, burns aside. Al-Ashrad is truly an ecception to the rule, even compared to his sire. Assuming he became Baali after the Embrace, I never heard or imagined that an Assamite turning Baali would change his skin condition.<br /> <br /> Al-Ashrad also sired childer and might be the ancestor of quite a few Assamite Sorcerers - their skin darkens as usual.<br /> <br /> I'd rather ascribe the non-blackening of al-Ashrad to his own magics than to other factors.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":16965,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","user":"Hermeticus","date":". 04-16-2024, 02:02 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-16T13:30:51-07:00","id":1534304,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CajunKhan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534073-why-does-assamite-skin-turn-darker-with-age?p=1534282#post1534282\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I thought I read somewhere that it was a result of their constant diablerie? Old memory so I'm not swearing by it, but I seem to recall reading that somewhere.</div> </div> </div> </div>That could work as a possible explaination for the darker skin tones.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-16T20:06:55-07:00","id":1534319,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534073-why-does-assamite-skin-turn-darker-with-age?p=1534288#post1534288\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> \u0130f you are saying that Banu Haqim, because of the darkening skin, are singled out among the jinn-vampires for being extra-evil...</div> </div> </div> </div>If you reduce it down to the bare essentials, honestly that's what I'm getting at. It's the one thing I can think of, which even approaches cogency when talking about the subject. The only rationale I can come up with for it, is it was Haqim's solution to the question of &quot;who watches the watchmen?&quot;. That is to say, for the array of supernatural means to influence the clan, control bodies and minds, available to any vampire let alone those of a clan who possess their own blood sorceries -- they need some means to police their own, independent of the clan's own leadership structure which could be (and was) corrupted.<br /> <br /> But like I said, that makes the optics even worse than they already were. In the final analysis, it's just better all around to just remove the phenomenon from the canon.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That would seem like a very silly decision of illustrators and whoever instructed them. </div> </div> </div>I'd bet money that was exactly the case. But still, it is what it is, and we're left with a massive clusterfuck to headcanon. Like you pointed out, the one, major, canon methuselah who actually does have dark skin (while the others don't) is ur-Shulgi, and he's the one who's almost certainly a Baali infiltrator who usurped the clan for his own purposes...<br /> <br /> At the bare minimum, it certainly seems that whether or not he's still in torpor, going by aesthetics alone Haqim's certainly picked a side -- and the &quot;loyalists&quot; ain't it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> \u0130 never heard of Zoroaster being conclusively called a false prophet myself, but \u0130 can imagine some people - especially ignorant - saying that. \u0130t's usually recommended not to make decisive statements on these things. </div> </div> </div>This is getting into the realm of tangent, but I'm honestly a bit surprised by this. Granted my formal education at the university level on Islam was limited (the majority of my classes were on Judaism), and my own contact with scholars on Islam equally so, but that's what I typically heard with regards to Zoroaster and Zoroastrianism in Islamic studies. But, that's from the religious academic perspective, as opposed to theological.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-18T12:49:03-07:00","id":1534405,"title":"","content":"If you want to have the Banu Haqim have darker skin in your game. The diablerie angle and or the blood sorcery angle could be a good reason for having dark veins or consuming the unworthy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-18T19:04:28-07:00","id":1534424,"title":"","content":"I think OOC it was done just to distinguish the Assamites from all other vampires. Someone thought it'd be cool if vampires became pale, then these would go the other way. Unfortunately it didn't seem to occur to the writer that the reason vampires are described as pale is because of a lack of blood, and simply turning the skin dark therefore seems strange.<br /> <br /> In terms of IC explanation, I just assumed it was part of Caine's curse like all other clan weaknesses. The Assamites are a clan of diablerists who routinely murdered other vampires. The color black is already associated with diablerie - it is mentioned that vampires with Auspex looking at the aura of vampires who have practiced diablerie appear with black veins in their aura. For a clan of diablerists, it makes sense that black stain will increase and even influence their physical appearance. The sins of the father (in this case the Assamite antedeluvian) will be visited upon the children (in this case all other Assamites). It is a good way to warn all other vampires of what this vampire is and the danger it represents. Since it is part of the curse of Caine, it doesn't matter if that particular Assamite ever engaged in diablerie. It's a way to make sure the clan can always be identified by other vampires, and since the darker the skin represents an older vampire, that means others can use to guage how much of a threat that Assamite could be.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-24T07:29:15-07:00","id":1534634,"title":"","content":"I wonder if this curse is limited to the epidermis alone or extends to other body tissues? (Do very ancient Assasmites see the whites of their eyes turn black and would their internal organs also turn black, for example).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-24T12:51:17-07:00","id":1534656,"title":"","content":"Interesting idea about the white of there eyes becoming black with age and making them more eerie for other kindred to deal with.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":27361,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","user":"Lysander","date":". 04-24-2024, 03:55 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-25T01:49:21-07:00","id":1534685,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Atticus Cleanclaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1534073-why-does-assamite-skin-turn-darker-with-age?p=1534634#post1534634\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I wonder if this curse is limited to the epidermis alone or extends to other body tissues? (Do very ancient Assasmites see the whites of their eyes turn black and would their internal organs also turn black, for example).</div> </div> </div> </div>To my memory, the books have consistently spoken of their <i>skin</i> turning black. So \u0130 wouldn't extend it to the other body tissues. Jet black eyes suit Lasombra and Kyasid better.<br /> Also, black teeth etc would really mess up Assamite chances at passing as human without Obfuscate, so they would turn into Nosferatus on a timer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-25T08:33:26-07:00","id":1534696,"title":"","content":"Well given this is explicitly a Curse we discuss, that it<br /> makes existence as an Assamite increasingly-difficult over time would be rather on-point.<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude00","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-04-25T15:44:05-07:00","id":1534740,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div cla